  shortAnswer="Pain on the bench is common enough to plan for, and it is not a reason to panic or to train through it. In a survey of 245 competitive powerlifters, 43 percent reported problems during routine workouts, and the shoulder, lower back and knee were the most commonly injured regions. Elbow and hand and wrist problems were more common in lifters over 40, and hand and wrist problems in women. We found no research linking forearm strength or wrist angle to bench pain, so this page does not offer fixes for them. If pain keeps coming back, gets worse, or comes with numbness or weakness, see a doctor or physical therapist."
